# ApexTrace

Driving signatures from public Formula 1 telemetry.

[![tests](https://github.com/SadMan-ZaMaN/ApexTrace/actions/workflows/tests.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/SadMan-ZaMaN/ApexTrace/actions/workflows/tests.yml)

![The ApexTrace overview page: two 2024 Abu Dhabi pole laps replayed on a track drawn from the timing feed](docs/images/overview.png)

I'm a CSE student at BUET and a big Formula 1 fan. After a signals and systems course I wanted to find out
whether you can recognise a driver from the free public timing data, and how much of what looks like a
driver's style really comes from the car.

ApexTrace looks at every braking zone of every qualifying lap from 2023 to 2025, and of 2026 up to a fixed
cutoff. For each one it measures where the driver starts braking, how much speed they keep through the slowest
point, and when they get back on the throttle. The catch is the data: it arrives only about {{ data.rate_hz }} times a
second, with gaps. So a big part of the project was working out how accurate each of those measurements can
be before trusting any of them.

I kept two seasons locked away while I worked. 2025 was used once, at the end, for a test I had written down and
committed beforehand. 2026, the first year of the new technical rules, got the same treatment.

## Results

| | Question | Result | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| H1 | Are corner features repeatable? | lap-level ICC {{ h1.lap_min }}–{{ h1.lap_max }} ({{ h1.lap_half }} of {{ h1.cells }} at 0.5 or more), weekend-level {{ h1.weekend_min }}–{{ h1.weekend_max }} | weak to moderate; includes the car |
| H2 | Do two teammates differ by more than the measurement error? (pilot) | {{ pilot.discoveries }} of {{ pilot.tests }} comparisons | not shown: too few laps, as predicted |
| H3 | Can corner features tell teammates apart on weekends the model never saw? | 2025: {{ h3.final.ours }} vs {{ h3.final.lap_time }} for lap time, {{ h3.final.wins }} of {{ h3.final.teams }} teams, {{ h3.final.p }} | **supported**, replicated on the held-out season |
| H4 | Does the style follow drivers who change teams? | 2025: {{ h4.final.own_closer }} of {{ h4.final.changers }} team changers, {{ h4.final.p }} | not supported |
| H5 | Does the style survive the 2026 rule change? | 2026: {{ h5.all.own }} of {{ h5.all.n }} drivers, {{ h5.all.p }} ({{ h5.needed }} needed) | {{ h5.verdict }}: can't tell weakened from lost |

**Teammates can be told apart.** Looking only at how two teammates take their corners, a model picks the right
driver on 2025 weekends it never saw with a balanced accuracy of {{ h3.final.ours }} (0.5 would be guessing), clearly better than
lap time alone. To be fair about what that shows: it separates a driver together with their side of the garage
(set-up, maybe parts, how far they got in qualifying), not the driver on their own. Across 2023 and 2024, a
driver's season signature stayed closer to their own previous season than to their teammate's for {{ h5.dev.first.own }} of {{ h5.dev.first.n }}
drivers ({{ h5.dev.first.p }}).

**Simple lap statistics do just as well.** Numbers like the share of the lap spent braking reach {{ h3.final.raw }}. The
corner measurements don't beat them. What they add is that they show *where* two drivers differ. Giving a
model both kinds of input helped only a little ({{ x21.dev.add_corner.diff }} in development, {{ x21.final.add_corner.diff }} in 2025), too little to be sure about.

**The style didn't clearly follow drivers to a new team.** Only {{ h4.final.own_closer }} of {{ h4.final.changers }} drivers who moved in 2025 stayed
closer to their old selves than to their new teammate. That "no" is weak evidence, though. Drivers who stayed in
their team passed the same comparison {{ h4.explore.control_own }} times out of {{ h4.explore.control_n }} in 2025, but only {{ h5.h4.dev_stayers_first }} from 2023 to 2024, so the
comparison itself leans towards the current teammate.

**It wasn't shown to survive the 2026 rule change either.** {{ h5.all.own }} of {{ h5.all.n }} drivers stayed closer to their own 2025
signature than to their 2025 teammate's; the test needed {{ h5.needed }}. Without a rule change the same comparison gave
{{ h5.dev.first.own }} of {{ h5.dev.first.n }} and {{ h5.dev.second.own }} of {{ h5.dev.second.n }}. I said before running it that this test couldn't tell a weaker signature from a
lost one: with {{ h5.all.n }} drivers it had only {{ h5.power_70 }} % power if the true share had dropped to 0.7. A rough check that takes
the car out (comparing teammates on the same weekends) points to "weaker": for {{ x26.rel.rules.positive }} of {{ x26.rel.rules.pairs }} teammate pairs, the
difference between them kept its direction across the rule change.

